<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yunnan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yunnan is a province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> bordering Myanmar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> as its capital. Its plateau was the base of the Nanzhao and Dali kingdoms until the Mongol conquest of 1253, after which it was ruled as a province of China. Home to more ethnic groups than any other Chinese province, it was the scene of the Panthay rebellion of 1856–1873.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yunnan.jpg" alt="Yunnan" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:55936-Lijiang_(27941831943).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony transformed the region’s infrastructure: the Haiphong–Kunming railway to <b>Yunnan</b> was completed in 1910, the Long Bien bridge across the Red River at Hanoi in 1902, and the Trans-Indochinese Railway from Hanoi to Saigon in 1936.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Overland trade with <b>Yunnan</b> in cotton and silk revived after the war with the Qing, and Rangoon grew as the main seaport until its loss to Britain in 1852.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiang Mai and other towns lay on caravan routes that linked <b>Yunnan</b> in south-western China to the Thai lowlands and Burma, along which traders carried goods such as silk, metalware, salt, tea and forest products.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Lan Xang was landlocked, and its exports travelled by river and overland trails to Ayutthaya, Cambodia, Vietnam and <b>Yunnan</b>: gold, benzoin, lac, ivory, musk and other forest goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Pagan grew out of the migration of Burman, or Bamar, peoples from the north into the dry zone of central Myanmar during the ninth century, after the Pyu city-states that had dominated the region were weakened, partly by raids from the <b>Yunnan</b> kingdom of Nanzhao.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In about the 2nd century BCE the Tibeto-Burman-speaking Pyu entered the Irrawaddy valley from present-day <b>Yunnan</b> by the Taping and Shweli rivers and founded settlements across the plains around the confluence of the Irrawaddy and Chindwin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
